[list]
[*]Rival Exchanges are separate from diplomatic rivalries again. Your Exchange rivals whichever Exchanges it likes, as many as it likes. A rivalry counts whichever side started it.
[*]Each rival costs influence maintenance by its Standing, from 25 to 150, like vanilla pacts. Rivaling costs 10 relations at once, then 1 a month down to Poor. A rivalry cannot be ended in its first year.
[*]The Leading Exchanges list has a rivalry button on each Exchange, in the style of vanilla's subsidize button: a silhouette while off, lit while you rival them. Its tooltip gives the maintenance and the relations cost. A small shield marks Exchanges that rival you.
[*]Rivalries are now a tug of war settled every 5 years. The bar starts from the two scores, and each action that works pushes it 10% your way, up to 5 pushes a side. It settles in five results: a draw near the middle, a win that takes trust from the loser, or a decisive win that takes twice as much plus 10% of the loser's investment pool income for a year.
[*]The Rivalries section opens with Rival Actions on Us: each Bear Raid or capital lure running on your market, who did it, what it does and the months left, and the last press campaign or failed raid against you within the year. Two tables follow with one layout: Country, Contest and Actions. You can act against Exchanges rivaling you too. Rivalries are started and ended from the Leading Exchanges list.
[*]The Contest bar is drawn like Market Confidence: it slides toward whoever leads, chevrons run toward whoever has pushed more, and the result it would settle at now is framed and named. Hover any of its five sections to see what that result would do to both Exchanges.
[*]Action buttons stay lit while they run on the target or wait out their cooldown, instead of going dark.
[*]New action Hold the Line undoes one of a rival's pushes against you.
[*]Shore Up is now per rival: it halves that rival's moves against you for 12 months, with a 2-year cooldown per rival. A shield after a rival's name means they have shored up against you.
[*]Rival actions scale with the two Exchanges' sizes: a larger rival costs more to act against and feels each action less, and a larger Exchange hits harder (half to double). Tooltips show the numbers for each rival.
[*]New icons for the rival moves, a painted picture for the rival event, and a new Speculative Excess alert icon.
[*]AI Exchanges pick rivals they can beat within one Standing, preferring higher Standings for the bigger prize, once a year: one rival, or two from International Standing. Early in a round they drop a rivalry they cannot win. They act more as a contest nears its end, shore up against whoever hit them, hold the line against your pushes, and save their money once safely ahead.
[*]Old saves: a rival named in 1.0.6 or beta.8 stays your rival. Beta.11 rivalries, which came from diplomacy, no longer count. Diplomatic rivalries are not changed.
[*]Capital fleeing a Financial Panic goes first to a rival Exchange, instead of a diplomatic rival.
[*]The Answer in kind choice of the rival event now names the action and shows its cost and effect.
[*]World Centre is now held by the top three Exchanges, not first place alone. A World Centre gets -10% loan interest, +10% influence, 30% smaller losses from foreign panics, and one rival free of upkeep.
[*]Every Exchange speciality now has a drawback, and it grows with your place: x1.25 at third, x1.5 at second and x2 at first. Busy Floor costs Landowners approval, Sound Lending Industrialists approval, Deep Pockets tax collection, and Open Door leverage resistance. Lender to Nations now costs investment pool efficiency instead of Industrialists approval. Some charter path effects are toned down.
[*]Long speciality names such as Home of Great Companies now shrink to fit instead of being cut off.
[/list]
